"Justin Hiltscher" <jdhilts@ubsi.com> wrote:

> Is this the correct format for patch?  Anyone know
> how to doctor it, if it  isn't?

What you have, and it says so, is a "normal diff";
not good enough.

You want to do a "context diff" instead, which I
think is "diff -c".  Without context, patch has to
work on _exactly_ the same code you modified, which
means that only one patch could ever be successfully
applied, in general. With context, patch can explore
a bit to match the context before trying to apply
your patch. I think the usual amount of context is
three lines above and below the change, which is
also controllable by a flag to diff, IIRC.

[Yes, I just went and checked the man() page for
diff; "diff -c" gets you three lines of context,
"diff -C nnn" gets you "nnn" lines of context
instead. Three usually suffices and I don't know
that patch will work with other amounts.]

